en has spoiled  language instruction  because of his insistence on input over  grammar instruction  but my impression in talking to  i don't know how many tens and tens of  teachers  was that lip service still is paid to  the idea of input and that the curricula  the school boards even the parents still  insist on more of a grammar-based method  of instruction which really isn't that  successful when they wander away from  that results improve i attended for  example a presentation by a group of  university teachers of japanese i think  it was smith college and  university of massachusetts amherst  and they had a program which in japanese  is known as tadoku like read a lot  and they measured the students and they  found that the students liked it and  that they spent a lot of time reading  rather than the more conventional  method of instruction that they ended up  doing better which is similar to the  research that krashen  and others have done to demonstrate the  advantage of an input based method of  instruction  um i went to amongst the different  places that i visited but it was very  exciting seven thousand people all of  them if it was a presentation in french  the teachers the french teachers many of  whom were  english-speaking uh americans  were very very fluent in french or in  spanish  again very exciting to be with with  people who are motivated and who are  committed to what they're doing  there was a presentation for example by  a group of korean teachers  uh talking about a program they'd set up  whereby students i think it was yale  princeton i don't know where else they  could connect with seniors in korea and  therefore converse in korean so i  listened to this in korea and then i  stood up in korean and said i'm a senior  but i'm learning korean can i be part of  the program and of course they said no  because you have to be a student  at one of their schools  uh what else was it there was a  presentation by the university of san  diego again by ms marianne  uh  lyman hagar whom i've often quoted  because of her insight into what i call  the three keys of language learning in  other words  uh you the attitude of the learner the  time with the language and your ability  to notice and they presented this  marvelous  um  interactive  uh multimedia presentation on french  civilization and explained how it would  be used in the classroom and stuff and  so there's lots of of interesting uh  presentations to go to  uh there were some i was listening to  one by a chinese professor explaining  the subtle differences between  this expression and that expression in  chinese and how the teacher could  explain this and all kinds of detail and  i felt that again that  really the differences are so subtle  that there's no way you can explain it  it's again through  massive input that you start to get a  feel for how these different  expressions are used  but language language is culture like  here again you know you think of french  civilization van gaal van gogh is of  course a dutch painter but he became  involved in the artistic scene in france  this is al in 1888  language connects us with so many  different things and i was experiencing  that  there in in boston speaking lots of  different languages i was surprised how  underrepresented russian as a language  was because it's one of the major  languages of the world  we had i met someone teaching swahili  the koreans were active latin and so  forth and really russian was not very  active at all i think that's really a  pity  i'm involved in organizing the  montreal language festival in august i  hope we do a better job of presenting  some of these other languages including  russian  uh farsi too one day for me i hope  um anyway that's basically it uh to me  input is the way to go that's not to say  that you don't spend some time reviewing  grammar with or without a teacher  uh that you don't have to eventually  output a lot you eventually have to  speak a lot but the way you ramp up your  skills you ramp up your familiarity with  the language you develop the potential  to speak well is through massive input  listening and reading and my experience  in boston confirmed that for me so thank  you for listening and that's all i have  to say and now i have to get back to  seriously get back to my korean  challenge bye for now
 

